//OUT="9 0 1 2 2"

// A `for` loop does not introduce a fresh binding: if the iterator name
// already exists — here a global — the loop writes to it, and the value it
// stopped at is still there afterwards. This follows the same rule as
// 07_deeper_indentation_shares_the_variable.cpl, where an assignment resolves
// outward to the nearest existing name, but it is easy to be caught by:
// `for i` in a function silently rewrites a global `i`.

i = 9

fn main
    out "{i} "
    for i 0..3
        out "{i} "
    out "{i}"
